--- !u!114 &2125573938
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2125573936}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f553d6ffa03804636a6e8eb25ce66cb9, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::SphereDialogue
  lines:
  - Have you ever tried a kakigori?
  - I once ordered one somewhere in Japan and was confused why the guy asked if I
    need more than one spoon.
  - Turned out that kakigori was so humongous that the guy was assuming I'm sharing
    it with somebody.
  - The word kakigori literally means shaved ice. It has a similar texture as the
    fruit sorbet we talked about earlier,
  - But the flavors are very different, since the frozen part is just ice. The tastes
    come from the topping syrup.
  - "Kakig\u014Dri began in Japan's Heian period as an exclusive luxury for the aristocracy,
    made by shaving winter ice stored in ice houses and topping it with sweet syrup."
  - It later became widely accessible to the public during the Meiji era in the late
    19th century as ice transport, shops, and shaving machines developed.
  - Now there is a large variation of sweeteners for kakigori, yet way back then
    the flavors are limited to arrowroot and honey.
  - Apparently Green Seafoo decided to theme this room around a chocolate flavor
    kakigori because of its similar color to wood planks.
  - Anyways, remember to finish your kakigori quickly since pure ice melts faster
    than those that have sugar and cream in their recipes!
